  "formulaSessionRpeLoadTitle": "Training load of a ride",
  "formulaSessionRpeLoadHow": "How hard the whole ride felt, from 0 to 10, times its length in minutes. A 60-minute ride you rate 5 counts as 300 points.",
  "formulaSessionRpeLoadLimits": "It measures how hard the ride felt to you, not your power or heart rate. Rate the whole ride, not its hardest moment.",
  "formulaLoadTrendTitle": "This week compared with your usual week",
  "formulaLoadTrendHow": "Your training load over the last 7 days, divided by your average week over the 4 weeks before them.",
  "formulaLoadTrendLimits": "Coaches often treat a week more than about one and a half times your usual week as a warning sign. That rule comes from team sports, and its link to injury is debated, so Ride Doctor only uses it to suggest an easier day.",
  "formulaCarbohydrateByDurationTitle": "Food during a ride",
  "formulaCarbohydrateByDurationHow": "Under an hour you usually need only water. From one to two and a half hours, aim for about 30 to 60 grams of carbohydrate an hour. Longer than that, about 60 to 90 grams an hour, if your stomach is used to it.",
  "formulaCarbohydrateByDurationLimits": "General guidance for endurance exercise, not a personal plan. Try new food and drink on training rides, never first in a race.",
  "formulaSweatRateTitle": "Your sweat rate",
  "formulaSweatRateHow": "Weight before the ride minus weight after, plus what you drank, minus any urine, divided by the hours you rode. One kilogram lost is about one litre of sweat.",
  "formulaSweatRateLimits": "It changes with heat, humidity and effort, so measure it in weather like your usual rides. Aim to lose no more than 2% of your weight on a ride, and do not drink so much that you gain weight.",
  "improveLogEveryRide": "Log every ride, easy ones too, so your weekly totals are complete.",
  "improveMeasureSweatRate": "Weigh yourself before and after a ride of an hour or more to measure your own sweat rate.",
  "improvePractiseFuelling": "Practise eating and drinking on training rides so your stomach gets used to it.",
